# CL-DFT-004-v1.0 — DRAFT: OPERATOR SOVEREIGNTY & OVERRIDE

**Status: DRAFT for operator adoption** (a §9 governance change — the operator's to adopt). Proposed as a clause of the graduated-autonomy governance (OP-PROT-008-R0.1).

## Principle
Dylan M. Roy is the operator and sole rule-maker. Every rule, gate, threshold, boundary, and fail-closed switch in this governance is a rule made **for the team (the model lines and automation), not a constraint on the operator.** The operator may waive, modify, suspend, or override any of them — for a single item or in general, temporarily or permanently — at his **sole discretion**, effective when he records it.

## The guardrail that *protects* this authority (not a limit on it)
This override authority belongs to the operator **alone**. It cannot be exercised, inferred, delegated, or actioned by any model, observer, relay, or automation on his behalf. It is invoked only by the operator's **direct authenticated decision** (per R0.1 §3.2) — the same channel that stops anyone from *forging* an override in his name. This is the one thing that keeps "only me" actually meaning only you.

## Form of an override (operator, direct entry)
```
OPERATOR OVERRIDE — [waive | modify | suspend] [rule/gate ID, or "all gates for <scope>"],
effective [now | until <date>]. Basis: operator discretion. — Dylan M. Roy
```
Recorded additively in the Operator Decisions log; prior state preserved and marked superseded (§10.4) — so any override is itself reversible by the operator.

## Note (a fact, not a rule)
The override is total over anything the system can roll back — which, given the additive/timestamped design, is nearly everything, and it's fully recoverable. The only actions no override can *undo* are those that leave the system: external release/publication, and results others build on downstream. Those stay reversible-in-authority but not reversible-in-effect — worth a glance before overriding there. Everything internal is fully recoverable.
